This problem is about permutations.

The number of ways to choose three people from five is the number of permutations of five things taken three at a time.

The number of permutations of five things taken three at a time is

\begin{align*}
\frac{5!}{(5-3)!}
&=
\frac{5!}{2!}
&=
\frac{5\cdot4\cdot3\cdot2\cdot1}{2\cdot1}
&=
5\cdot4\cdot3
&=
60
\end{align*}

Therefore, there are \boxed{30} different groups of three that Jenna and her friends can make.

