The floor is tiled with square tiles of size 1 foot by 1 foot. Each tile has a pattern consisting of four white quarter circles of radius 1/2 foot centered at each corner of the tile. The remaining portion of the tile is shaded.

The area of a square tile is 1 square foot. The area of a quarter circle of radius 1/2 foot is \frac{1}{4}\pi\left(1/2\right)^2=\frac{1}{4}\pi\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)=\frac{1}{16}\pi\text{ square feet}.

The area of the shaded portion of a tile is 1 square foot minus the area of the four quarter circles, which is 1-\frac{1}{4}\pi\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)=1-\frac{1}{16}\pi\text{ square feet}.

There are 64 square tiles in the floor, so the total area of the shaded portion of the floor is 64\left(1-\frac{1}{16}\pi\right)=64-\frac{64}{16}\pi=64-\frac{4}{16}\pi=64-\frac{1}{4}\pi=63\frac{1}{4}\pi\text{ square feet}.

Therefore, the final answer is \boxed{\frac{15}{4}}.

